Dear Editor-in-Chief,

I am pleased to submit the manuscript entitled

\begin{center}
\itshape ``Method-Induced Uncertainty in Gear Bending Fatigue: Factorial\\
Decomposition of Size-and-Surface Factor Sensitivity Under a\\
Common ISO Stress Baseline''
\end{center}

for consideration for publication as an original research article in
the \textit{Journal of Mechanical Science and Technology}.

The manuscript quantifies, for the first time, how much of the scatter
in gear bending-fatigue life estimates is inherited from the analyst's
methodological choices rather than from the material itself. A
full-factorial design of 144 combinations of size and surface factors
under a common ISO stress baseline is decomposed with an analysis of
variance: among the 144 combinations, 84 are run-outs and 60 reach
finite life. The variance decomposition attributes the uncertainty to
six contributions (55.3\%, 22.6\%, 18.2\%, 2.5\%, 1.2\%, and 0.3\%),
with bootstrap resampling (2000 resamples, seed 42) confirming the
stability of the ranking. A Shapiro--Wilk test (W$=$0.962, $p=0.056$)
supports the normality assumption underlying the analysis, and a
model-selection comparison (AIC) yields $\Delta$AIC$=+77.1$ in favour
of the proposed specification. The paper reports 11 figures and 6
tables.

We believe the results are directly relevant to JMST's readership in
mechanical design and fatigue assessment: they show that a large
fraction of reported fatigue-life scatter can be traced to factor
choice and calibration, and they provide a reproducible
variance-decomposition workflow (Python scripts, seed-fixed
randomness, and a full reproducibility package) that any
fatigue-engineering laboratory can apply to its own data.

This manuscript is original, has not been published previously, and is
not under consideration by any other journal. The author declares no
competing interests.

Generative AI tools assisted with LaTeX formatting, Python code
implementation, and English language editing.

Thank you for your consideration.

\vspace{4mm}

